  "text": "Solution 2 is wrong. Take disjoint copies of the regular tournament $T_5$ (vertices $\\mathbb{Z}_5$, arcs $i\\to i+1$ and $i\\to i+2$). Each vertex has total degree 4, so $\\Delta=4$. Any single deletion leaves a 4-vertex tournament with score sequence $(2,2,1,1)$, which is not transitive and so contains a directed 3-cycle. Deleting $\\{0,1\\}$ leaves a transitive tournament, so $fvs(T_5)=2$. Disjoint copies therefore give $fvs/n = 2/5 > 1/3$, which rules out $c^*=1/3$.\n\nSolution 1's lower bound $c^*\\ge 2/5$ is correct by the $T_5$ construction. Its upper-bound argument is not valid: a proper 5-colouring does not give a homomorphism to $T_5$, so the claim that every arc satisfies $\\phi(v)-\\phi(u)\\in\\{1,2\\}$ is unjustified. I believe $2/5$ is the known tight value for maximum degree 4, but I could not verify that from the literature here, so the final value rests on recall rather than the proof given.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
